# Active Record Patterns

This project does **not** use fat `ApplicationRecord` domain models or `app/services/`.

Persistence lives in `*::Record` classes. Domain orchestration lives in `ApplicationSolidProcess` under `app/models/core/`. See `AGENTS.md` for the full layer map.

## Records

```ruby
class User::Record < ApplicationRecord
  self.table_name = "users"

  has_many :sessions, class_name: "User::Session::Record", foreign_key: :user_id, dependent: :destroy

  has_secure_password
end
```

Rules:

- Name persistence classes `*::Record` (never expose them as the public domain model).
- Always set `self.table_name` when the class name does not match the table.
- Always declare `dependent:` on associations that own child records.
- Prefer `class_name:` + `foreign_key:` for nested record namespaces.
- Keep Records thin: associations, `has_secure_password`, `generates_token_for`, and DB-level concerns only.
- Do **not** put use-case orchestration, mail delivery, or multi-step workflows in Records — use solid-process.

## Mappers

Convert between Records and domain entities:

```ruby
module User::Mapper
  extend self

  def to_entity(record)
    return if record.nil?

    Core::User::Entity.new(
      id: record.id,
      first_name: record.first_name,
      last_name: record.last_name,
      email: record.email,
      verified: record.verified,
      active: record.active
    )
  end

  def to_record(entity)
    User::Record.find(entity.id)
  end

  def to_errors(record)
    Core::Errors.new(record.errors.messages)
  end
end
```

Adapters under `app/models/<context>/**/adapters/` talk to Records and return entities / `Solid::Success` / `Solid::Failure`. Controllers and processes must not reach into Records when an adapter/facade exists.

## Associations & queries

- Use scopes on Records for simple, reusable filters.
- Prevent N+1 with `includes` / `preload` when reading associations.
- Use `joins` when filtering without loading associated records.
- Prefer `find_each` for large batches.

```ruby
# Bad — N+1
User::Record.all.each { |user| user.sessions.count }

# Good
User::Record.includes(:sessions)
```

## Callbacks

Avoid callbacks for business logic and side effects.

```ruby
# Avoid — side effect buried in the Record
class User::Record < ApplicationRecord
  after_create :send_welcome_email
end

# Prefer — explicit orchestration in a solid-process (see Core::User::Registration)
```

## Migrations

```ruby
class CreateUsers < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]
  def change
    create_table :users, id: :uuid do |t|
      t.string :first_name, null: false
      t.string :last_name, null: false
      t.string :email, null: false, index: { unique: true }
      t.string :password_digest, null: false
      t.boolean :verified, null: false, default: false
      t.boolean :active, null: false, default: true

      t.timestamps
    end
  end
end
```

- Prefer UUID primary keys (project standard via `pgcrypto`).
- Add `null:` and sensible `default:` constraints at the DB level.
- Add indexes for columns used in `where` / `order` / joins and unique business keys.
- In data migrations, prefer `find_each`; `update_column(s)` skips validations, callbacks, and timestamps — use deliberately.

## Concerns

Use only for genuinely shared, cohesive persistence behavior — not as a dumping ground for domain logic.

## Performance

- Add DB indexes for frequently queried columns.
- Use `select` to limit columns; `pluck` instead of `map` for single attributes.
- Use `find_each` for large datasets.
